Patch 1/6 is a trivial, well-reviewed os_ok whitelist addition - fine.

Patch 2/6 (common/spl/spl_ram.c) has a shadowing issue:

  if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SPL_LOAD_FIT) &&
      image_get_magic(header) == FDT_MAGIC) {
          debug("Found FIT\n");
          /* by default, assume failure and fall back to simple FIT */
          int ret = -1;
          ...
  } else {

The function already has an outer-scope `ret` (used by the pre-existing
`ret = spl_load_simple_fit(...)` pattern and presumably the function's
return value). Declaring `int ret = -1;` inside this if-block shadows that
outer variable. If the compiler's -Wshadow is enabled this will warn, and
more importantly if any code further down in the function (after this
if/else) reads the outer `ret` expecting it to reflect the FIT load result,
it will now see stale/uninitialized data instead, since the inner `ret` is
scoped only to the if-block. Please rename the inner variable (e.g.
`fit_ret`) or declare it at the top of the function alongside the existing
`ret`.

Also: when CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(LOAD_FIT_FULL) is not set, `ret` is left at -1
and unconditionally falls through to the simple-FIT path - that's correct
behavior, but worth a one-line comment since it's not obvious at a glance
that the "assume failure" default is what drives the fallback.
